---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
// Short install overview. The full, maintained steps live in
// docs/deployment.md and docs/truenas.md; this page links there
// instead of copying them, so the two cannot drift.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { DEMO_URL, repoFile } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base
  title="Install GratisGIS"
  description="Install GratisGIS on one Linux host with Docker Compose, or on a home or office NAS. Requirements, the one-line installer, and where the full guides live."
  path="/install/"
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Install</p>
    <h1>Install GratisGIS</h1>
    <p>
      GratisGIS runs as one Docker Compose stack on one Linux host. This page is a short overview.
      The full guide, with backups, health checks and recovery, is{" "}
      <a href={repoFile('docs/deployment.md')}>docs/deployment.md</a> on GitHub.
    </p>

    <h2>What you need</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A Linux server you can SSH into. The public demo runs on 4 CPU cores and 8 GB RAM, which is
        a comfortable baseline for a small organization. Plan for 20 GB or more of free disk.
      </li>
      <li>Docker Engine with the Compose v2 plugin, plus git, curl, openssl and python3.</li>
      <li>
        Three DNS records pointing at the server: the portal, an auth host and a storage host. For
        example <code>gis.example.org</code>, <code>auth.gis.example.org</code> and{" "}
        <code>storage.gis.example.org</code>. Certificates come from Let's Encrypt.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Install with one command</h2>
    <div class="code-panel" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Install command">
      <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/\
  palavido-dev/gratis-gis/main/infra/install.sh | bash</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      The installer clones the repository to <code>/opt/gratis-gis</code>, checks out the newest
      release, asks for your domain and email, generates the secrets, deploys, and prints where to
      sign in. It checks for Docker but does not install it for you.
    </p>
    <p>Prefer to see each step? The same flow by hand:</p>
    <div class="code-panel" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Manual install commands">
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/palavido-dev/gratis-gis /opt/gratis-gis
cd /opt/gratis-gis
./infra/setup.sh
./infra/deploy.sh
./infra/doctor.sh</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h2>On a home or office NAS</h2>
    <p>
      On TrueNAS SCALE 25.10 or newer, or Dockge, you paste one Compose file. No domain name is
      needed. Steps are in <a href={repoFile('docs/truenas.md')}>docs/truenas.md</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Upgrades</h2>
    <p>
      Run <code>./infra/deploy.sh</code> again. It moves to the newest release, applies database
      migrations and swaps containers. Back up first. The full procedure, including rollback, is in{" "}
      <a href={repoFile('docs/UPGRADING.md')}>docs/UPGRADING.md</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Working on the code</h2>
    <p>
      To run GratisGIS on a workstation for development, follow{" "}
      <a href={repoFile('docs/SETUP.md')}>docs/SETUP.md</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href={DEMO_URL}>Try the live demo first</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary btn-lg" href={repoFile('docs/deployment.md')}>Read the full guide</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
